{
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    /**
     * A Next application is a non-emitting project, and TypeScript refuses to
     * make one a `tsc -b` project reference: "a referenced project may not
     * disable emit" (TS6310). So this config is deliberately *not* referenced
     * from the solution root, and it is not what `npm run check` runs.
     *
     * Two consumers instead, which between them cover the app twice over:
     *
     *   1. `tsc -b` from the root, whose `include` names `apps/ledger/lib/**` and
     *      `apps/ledger/test/**`. That is the check wired into `npm run check`,
     *      and it runs under the repository's no-DOM `lib` — which is a feature:
     *      `lib/tokens.ts` and the source scans have no business touching the
     *      DOM, and this is what proves they do not.
     *   2. `tsc -p apps/ledger --noEmit`, for the browser-facing half. It adds
     *      `jsx` and the DOM libs that a React component needs and that the root
     *      deliberately withholds, and writes nothing: no `dist`, no build info,
     *      no artefact for `.gitignore` to chase.
     *
     * The overrides below are exactly the difference between a Node library and a
     * browser app: `composite`/`declaration` off because nothing references this
     * project, `noEmit` on, `jsx` for React 19's automatic runtime, and the DOM
     * libs. Everything strict in `tsconfig.base.json` — `verbatimModuleSyntax`,
     * `isolatedModules`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` — is inherited untouched.
     *
     * The `include` list names the `app` tree's plain `.ts` files as well as its
     * `.tsx` ones, because a route handler carries no JSX and is therefore not a
     * `.tsx` file: without that entry `app/badge.svg/route.ts` would be checked by
     * neither program — the root project names only the `lib` and `test` trees —
     * and an unchecked route handler is the one file in the application whose
     * exported signature is a public contract (R9.4).
     *
     * `lib/**\/*.tsx` is listed for the same reason `test/**\/*.tsx` is. A `lib`
     * module that is *about* the browser — `lib/motion.tsx`, the `animejs` gate of
     * §10.6.4 — cannot be checked by the root project, whose whole point for this
     * tree is a no-DOM `lib`. The extension moves it here, where the DOM libs are,
     * and leaves the root's guarantee over every other `lib` module intact.
     *
     * Module resolution stays `NodeNext` to match the root, so a relative import
     * carries its `.js` extension in both programs and neither disagrees with the
     * other about a specifier. Vite resolves those specifiers to the `.ts` source
     * when Vitest runs the suites.
     */
    "composite": false,
    "declaration": false,
    "declarationMap": false,
    "noEmit": true,
    "incremental": false,
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "types": ["node"]
  },
  "include": [
    "lib/**/*.ts",
    "lib/**/*.tsx",
    "test/**/*.ts",
    "test/**/*.tsx",
    "app/**/*.ts",
    "app/**/*.tsx",
    "components/**/*.tsx"
  ],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", ".next", "out"]
}
